========================================================================
PATTERN 5: HAGEDORN TEMPERATURE = CAYLEY MIDPOINT
========================================================================

  The rapidity gas Z(beta) = zeta(beta/2) diverges at beta=2, i.e., T=1/2.
  In the Cayley framework:
    T_H = 1/2, Cayley address = (1/2-1)/(1/2+1) = -0.333333
    But as a Q-value: Q(0) = 1 (the identity/unison)
    rapidity(T_H) = ln(1/2)/2 = -0.346574

  The Hagedorn temperature of the rapidity gas corresponds to
  rapidity = ln(1/2)/2 = -ln(2)/2 = minus one octave.
  Below this temperature: the 'gas' is ordered (zeta converges).
  Above this temperature: the 'gas' condenses (zeta diverges).

  CROSS-DOMAIN: The same T=1/2 boundary appears in:
  - Thermodynamics: Hagedorn temperature of rapidity gas
  - Information theory: fair coin bias (p=1/2, rapidity=0)
  - Quantum mechanics: 50/50 superposition on Bloch sphere
  - Tournament theory: random tournament arc probability

