========================================================================
PATTERN 13: RAPIDITY BINARY TREE vs STERN-BROCOT TREE
========================================================================

  The rapidity composition creates a binary tree:
    compose(2k, 2k+1) = k  for all k >= 1
  Children of node k: {2k, 2k+1}
  This is the standard binary tree on positive integers!

  The Stern-Brocot tree also generates all positive rationals
  via a binary tree structure, with mediant operation.
  The rapidity tree generates all musical intervals 1/(2n+1)
  via Einstein velocity addition.

  Both trees:
    - Are complete binary trees
    - Generate a dense subset of (0,1)
    - Each node determined uniquely by its position
    - Parent-child relationship via algebraic operation

  Differences:
    - Stern-Brocot uses MEDIANT: (a/b, c/d) -> (a+c)/(b+d)
    - Rapidity uses EINSTEIN: (v1, v2) -> (v1+v2)/(1+v1*v2)
    - Stern-Brocot generates ALL rationals; rapidity generates 1/(2n+1)

  Tree verification (first 4 levels):
    Level 0: [1]
    Level 1: [2, 3]
      compose(2, 3) = 2*3/(2+3+1) = 6/6 = 1
      compose(3, 2) = 3*2/(3+2+1) = 6/6 = 1
    Level 2: [4, 5, 6, 7]
      compose(4, 5) = 4*5/(4+5+1) = 20/10 = 2
      compose(5, 4) = 5*4/(5+4+1) = 20/10 = 2
      compose(6, 7) = 6*7/(6+7+1) = 42/14 = 3
      compose(7, 6) = 7*6/(7+6+1) = 42/14 = 3
    Level 3: [8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14, 15]
      compose(8, 9) = 8*9/(8+9+1) = 72/18 = 4
      compose(9, 8) = 9*8/(9+8+1) = 72/18 = 4
      compose(10, 11) = 10*11/(10+11+1) = 110/22 = 5
      compose(11, 10) = 11*10/(11+10+1) = 110/22 = 5
      compose(12, 13) = 12*13/(12+13+1) = 156/26 = 6
      compose(13, 12) = 13*12/(13+12+1) = 156/26 = 6
      compose(14, 15) = 14*15/(14+15+1) = 210/30 = 7
      compose(15, 14) = 15*14/(15+14+1) = 210/30 = 7

