PATTERN 15: RAMANUJAN'S 1729 AND THE TOURNAMENT QUOTIENT
========================================================================

  1729 = 12^3 + 1 = 10^3 + 9^3 (Hardy-Ramanujan / taxicab number)
  1729 = 7 * 13 * 19

  In tournament theory:
    H(T_7) = 189, |Aut(T_7)| = 21
    H(T_7) / |Aut(T_7)| = 189/21 = 9

  CORRECTION: The value 1729 appears as H/|Aut| for T_11:
    H(T_11) = 95095, |Aut(T_11)| = 55
    H(T_11)/|Aut(T_11)| = 95095/55 = 1729

    95095/55 = 1729.0 = 1729 remainder 0
    So H(T_11)/|Aut(T_11)| = 1729: YES

    1729 = 7 * 13 * 19
    Verify: 7 * 13 * 19 = 1729
    These are primes 4, 6, 8 (i.e., p_4, p_6, p_8 — EVEN-indexed primes)

    1729 = 12^3 + 1^3 = 10^3 + 9^3
    Verify: 12^3 + 1 = 1729, 10^3 + 9^3 = 1729

  WHY DOES RAMANUJAN'S NUMBER APPEAR IN TOURNAMENTS?
    H(T_11)/|Aut(T_11)| counts orbits of Hamiltonian paths under
    the automorphism group. The orbit count being a famous
    number-theoretic object (taxicab) suggests a deep connection
    between tournament symmetry and additive number theory.

